1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,076
October 27, 1962.

2
00:00:04,536 --> 00:00:09,694
In the Sargasso Sea, a Soviet submarine is trapped beneath a ring of American

3
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:10,301
warships.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:15,399
Above it, the Navy is dropping explosives to force it to the surface.

5
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:20,950
Inside, the air is running out, and no one on board knows whether the Third World

6
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:22,344
War has already begun.

7
00:00:23,617 --> 00:00:29,075
In its torpedo room is a weapon the Americans know nothing about: a torpedo with a

8
00:00:29,115 --> 00:00:30,148
nuclear warhead.

9
00:00:31,417 --> 00:00:33,722
Three officers have to agree to fire it.

10
00:00:34,182 --> 00:00:37,172
According to the men who were there, two of them were ready.

11
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:50,147
Two weeks earlier, an American U-2 spy plane photographed something new in the hills

12
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:55,918
of western Cuba: launch sites for Soviet nuclear missiles, less than a hundred miles

13
00:00:55,958 --> 00:00:56,811
from Florida.

14
00:00:58,083 --> 00:01:02,141
On October 22, President John Kennedy told the nation.

15
00:01:03,697 --> 00:01:08,467
a strict quarantine on all offensive military equipment under shipment to Cuba is

16
00:01:08,507 --> 00:01:09,417
being initiated.

17
00:01:10,483 --> 00:01:14,756
Soviet freighters were already at sea, some of them carrying missiles on their

18
00:01:14,796 --> 00:01:15,087
decks.

19
00:01:15,547 --> 00:01:16,285
The U.S.

20
00:01:16,745 --> 00:01:18,191
Navy filmed them from the air.

21
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:21,360
And Kennedy made one more promise.

22
00:01:22,837 --> 00:01:28,746
it shall be the policy of this nation to regard any nuclear missile launched from

23
00:01:28,786 --> 00:01:34,768
Cuba against any nation in the Western Hemisphere as an attack by the Soviet Union

24
00:01:34,808 --> 00:01:40,717
on the United States requiring a full retaliatory response upon the Soviet Union.

25
00:01:44,783 --> 00:01:48,930
What Washington did not know was that the Soviet Union had also sent four

26
00:01:48,970 --> 00:01:49,561
submarines.

27
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:54,870
On October 1, they had slipped out of their base on the Kola Peninsula, bound for

28
00:01:54,910 --> 00:01:55,171
Cuba.

29
00:01:56,417 --> 00:02:00,800
They were diesel-electric Foxtrot boats, built for the cold waters of the North.

30
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:03,356
Each carried twenty-two torpedoes.

31
00:02:03,817 --> 00:02:09,258
One of them, on every boat, had a nuclear warhead, roughly as powerful as the bomb

32
00:02:09,298 --> 00:02:10,929
that destroyed Hiroshima.

33
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:17,416
The Americans hunted them with everything they had: destroyers, aircraft carriers,

34
00:02:17,456 --> 00:02:20,502
patrol planes and underwater listening stations.

35
00:02:20,962 --> 00:02:25,403
In the Atlantic, most of the Navy's anti-submarine forces joined the search.

36
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:31,860
To make the submarines come up, Defense Secretary Robert McNamara approved a new

37
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:37,371
signal: practice depth charges, explosives the size of hand grenades, dropped on the

38
00:02:37,411 --> 00:02:38,487
water above them.

39
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:41,463
The signal was sent to Moscow.

40
00:02:41,923 --> 00:02:44,600
There is no evidence that it ever reached the submarines.

41
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:48,248
To the men below, a depth charge was a depth charge.

42
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:54,175
In the White House, on October 24, the President asked his advisers: if this

43
00:02:54,215 --> 00:02:58,440
submarine should sink our destroyer, then what is our proposed reply?

44
00:02:59,717 --> 00:03:03,680
His brother Robert wrote that those few minutes were the time of greatest worry for

45
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:04,356
the President.

46
00:03:04,816 --> 00:03:07,911
His hand went up to his face, and he closed his fist.

47
00:03:12,183 --> 00:03:16,996
Saturday, October 27, was the most dangerous day of the crisis.

48
00:03:17,456 --> 00:03:23,092
That morning, a Soviet missile shot down a U-2 over Cuba and killed its pilot, Major

49
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:24,173
Rudolf Anderson.

50
00:03:25,417 --> 00:03:29,754
In Washington, the Joint Chiefs were pressing for air strikes and an invasion.

51
00:03:30,214 --> 00:03:34,632
And in the Sargasso Sea, the hunters had found submarine B-59.

52
00:03:35,883 --> 00:03:39,303
The aircraft carrier Randolph and a group of destroyers surrounded it.

53
00:03:39,763 --> 00:03:43,427
The deck log of the USS Beale records what happened next.

54
00:03:44,683 --> 00:03:49,090
Dropped five hand grenades as challenge to submarine for identification.

55
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:50,474
No response.

56
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:55,205
Inside B-59, the batteries were almost dead.

57
00:03:55,665 --> 00:03:57,564
Only the emergency lights were on.

58
00:03:58,023 --> 00:04:02,726
In the compartments, the temperature was between forty-five and fifty degrees

59
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:03,219
Celsius.

60
00:04:04,483 --> 00:04:07,589
The level of carbon dioxide was close to lethal.

61
00:04:08,049 --> 00:04:10,180
Men began to faint at their posts.

62
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:14,083
They were falling, one officer remembered, like dominoes.

63
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:17,806
The explosions went off right next to the hull.

64
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:22,740
It felt, he said, like sitting in a metal barrel while somebody pounds it with a

65
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:23,474
sledgehammer.

66
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:27,020
For four hours, they tried to escape.

67
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:29,739
They had not heard from Moscow in days.

68
00:04:30,199 --> 00:04:33,259
For all they knew, the war had already started above them.

69
00:04:37,517 --> 00:04:40,705
Then the submarine was shaken by something stronger.

70
00:04:41,165 --> 00:04:46,640
The captain, Valentin Savitsky, exhausted and unable to reach the General Staff,

71
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:47,743
lost his temper.

72
00:04:49,017 --> 00:04:54,575
According to Vadim Orlov, the intelligence officer on board, Savitsky ordered the

73
00:04:54,615 --> 00:04:57,754
nuclear torpedo assembled to battle readiness.

74
00:04:59,017 --> 00:05:01,926
Maybe the war has already started up there, he shouted.

75
00:05:02,386 --> 00:05:03,774
We're going to blast them now.

76
00:05:04,234 --> 00:05:06,330
We will die, but we will sink them all.

77
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:08,399
We will not disgrace our Navy.

78
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:14,840
On B-59, the special weapon could only be launched if three officers agreed: the

79
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:20,005
captain, the political officer, Ivan Maslennikov, and the chief of staff of the

80
00:05:20,045 --> 00:05:23,797
whole submarine flotilla, who happened to be on this boat.

81
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:27,042
His name was Vasily Arkhipov.

82
00:05:27,502 --> 00:05:28,867
He was thirty-six.

83
00:05:29,327 --> 00:05:35,061
A year earlier, he had survived the reactor accident on the nuclear submarine K-19,

84
00:05:35,101 --> 00:05:38,122
which killed eight men in a matter of weeks.

85
00:05:39,383 --> 00:05:43,070
By the accounts of those who were there, Arkhipov was the one who said no.

86
00:05:43,530 --> 00:05:47,723
He calmed the captain down, and persuaded him to surface and wait for orders from

87
00:05:47,763 --> 00:05:48,088
Moscow.

88
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:53,861
At ten minutes to nine that evening, the Beale's log has one more entry.

89
00:05:54,321 --> 00:05:58,786
Submarine, identified as U.S.S.R. type Foxtrot, surfaced.

90
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:04,626
American planes from the Randolph swept over it with searchlights and took

91
00:06:04,666 --> 00:06:05,374
photographs.

92
00:06:05,834 --> 00:06:08,580
The crew climbed onto the bridge, into the night air.

93
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:12,829
The Americans had no idea what that submarine was carrying.

94
00:06:17,083 --> 00:06:21,151
The next morning, Nikita Khrushchev announced that the missiles would be removed

95
00:06:21,191 --> 00:06:21,664
from Cuba.

96
00:06:22,124 --> 00:06:23,210
The crisis was over.

97
00:06:24,483 --> 00:06:28,239
When the crew of B-59 got home, there were no medals.

98
00:06:28,699 --> 00:06:32,495
One admiral told them it would have been better if they had gone down with their

99
00:06:32,535 --> 00:06:32,734
ship.

100
00:06:33,983 --> 00:06:37,449
Arkhipov kept serving and became a vice admiral.

101
00:06:37,909 --> 00:06:39,518
He rarely spoke about that night.

102
00:06:39,978 --> 00:06:41,923
He died in 1998.

103
00:06:43,183 --> 00:06:48,193
Four years later, in Havana, American and Soviet veterans of the crisis met for its

104
00:06:48,233 --> 00:06:49,470
fortieth anniversary.

105
00:06:49,930 --> 00:06:53,617
Only then did the story of B-59 become widely known.

106
00:06:54,883 --> 00:06:59,835
Robert McNamara, Kennedy's Secretary of Defense, said they had come very, very

107
00:06:59,875 --> 00:07:02,331
close, closer than we knew at the time.

108
00:07:02,791 --> 00:07:08,132
The director of the National Security Archive called Arkhipov the man who saved the

109
00:07:08,172 --> 00:07:08,521
world.

110
00:07:12,783 --> 00:07:14,659
Not everything in this story is certain.

111
00:07:15,119 --> 00:07:16,855
Orlov's account is disputed.

112
00:07:17,315 --> 00:07:21,083
Other submarine commanders doubted that Savitsky would have given that order.

113
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:25,166
Most of what we know comes from the memories of the men on board.

114
00:07:25,626 --> 00:07:30,067
The Russian military documents released in 2022 do not mention it.

115
00:07:31,317 --> 00:07:33,018
But some facts are not in doubt.

116
00:07:33,478 --> 00:07:34,936
The torpedo was real.

117
00:07:35,396 --> 00:07:37,225
The Americans did not know it was there.

118
00:07:37,685 --> 00:07:38,527
And the U.S.

119
00:07:38,988 --> 00:07:41,549
Navy had nuclear depth charges of its own.

120
00:07:42,817 --> 00:07:47,710
One nuclear explosion in the Sargasso Sea, on the most dangerous day of the Cold

121
00:07:47,750 --> 00:07:50,486
War, could have been answered with many more.

122
00:07:51,830 --> 00:07:57,717
We will not prematurely or unnecessarily risk the course of worldwide nuclear war in

123
00:07:57,757 --> 00:08:01,950
which even the fruits of victory would be ashes in our mouth

124
00:08:03,017 --> 00:08:10,001
On October 27, 1962, the decision did not belong to a president or a premier.

125
00:08:10,461 --> 00:08:14,659
It came down to three men in a dark steel tube, and one of them said no.

126
00:08:15,917 --> 00:08:18,199
What else is still hidden in the files?

127
00:08:18,659 --> 00:08:22,864
If you want more declassified stories like this one, subscribe to Stunning

128
00:08:22,904 --> 00:08:23,553
Revelations.
